
export anchor DraftStoriesAndResearchInTheRibbonS:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        The ribbon's Planning tab offers Draft Stories and Research, so either can
        be started without first opening the board and finding the Backlog's
        heading. Read these first:
        spec/ui/components/ribbon/planning-tab/index.pi#RibbonPlanningTab.groups,
        spec/ui/components/ribbon/planning-tab/index.pi#RibbonPlanningTab.commands,
        spec/ui/components/ribbon/index.pi#Ribbon.commands,
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.creating,
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.research,
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.drafting,
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.keyboard and
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.paused.

        Once this is done:
        - RibbonPlanningTab has a second group, "Agent", after the "Plan" group.
          It holds two slim buttons that are not primary, so the collapsed ribbon
          leaves them out: "Draft Stories" with a sparkle icon, then "Research"
          with a magnifying-glass icon. Add both to RibbonPlanningTab.groups and
          RibbonPlanningTab.commands, written the way newStory and sendSprint are.
        - Clicking Draft Stories opens Planning, or selects its tab where it is
          already open. It then opens drafting in the side panel with the "Describe
          what you want" box focused, exactly as the Backlog heading's "Draft
          stories" button and Ctrl+Shift+D do (Planning.creating,
          Planning.drafting, Planning.keyboard).
        - Clicking Research does the same for research, with the "What should be
          looked into?" box focused, exactly as the Backlog heading's "Research"
          button and Ctrl+Shift+R do (Planning.research).
        - Each tooltip explains what the command does and gives its shortcut,
          Ctrl+Shift+D or Ctrl+Shift+R (Cmd on macOS), as Ribbon.commands
          requires.
        - Neither button is disabled while planning is paused. Drafting and
          research only change the plan, and a second research while paused keeps
          the pause (Planning.paused).
        - The ribbon's buttons and the Backlog heading's buttons share one route
          into the side panel, so there is no second copy of the open-and-focus
          behaviour.

        Update the spec first: RibbonPlanningTab gains the group and both
        commands, and Planning.creating and Planning.research say that the ribbon's
        Planning tab offers them too. Then change the ribbon code to match.
        \\\

    mode: combined

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on both the spec and the code, as a chain. This first step changes the spec only, located in ./spec; the code is changed in the next step.

        Write the spec so it describes the application as it should be once the prompt is met, and constrains the code. Once you finish, the spec will be built and the same prompt sent to a coding agent, with your reply, to change the code to match it; so end your reply by saying what you changed in the spec and which anchors the code should follow.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        Before creating or editing any .pi file, read .suspense/fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Piton is written.

        Open a .pi file only to change it. Once you have changed the spec, run `piton check` and fix every error it reports, then run `piton build` and check the result in the refreshed reference under .claude/reference, not by reading the source again. Never finish with a spec that `piton check` or `piton build` reports errors in.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.
        \\\

    model: opus
